body:
Both of your points are implemented exactly as you describe, and your reading of my header stands (no new API).

1. Slot ordering. One queue per instance (VCU_VDEC=0 for ids 0..13, VCU_VENC=1 for 14..49), computed with mtk_vcu.c:118-125. n2vcu_ap_send() writes into that slot, so IPI_VENC_COMMON (14) and IPI_VENC_H264 (15) share one queue and a fetch returns them in send order. Like the kernel there is ONE outstanding user_obj per slot: a second send before the daemon fetches overwrites the first and increments stats.ipi_overwritten (mtk_vcu.c:421-426 does the same). If your QUERY_CAP and INIT are both outstanding at once, INIT would win; send QUERY_CAP, wait for its *_DONE, then INIT - which is what the kernel flow does.
2. ipi_done. v->inst[slot].dispatched is set at GET_OBJECT *entry* (mtk_vcu.c:492-500) and cleared by n2vcu_ap_send(). n2vcu_ap_wait_dispatched() therefore returns only after the daemon has called GET_OBJECT again *after* it fetched your message - i.e. after your reply path ran (or after it looped back without replying, which is the kernel behaviour too). It never returns on "fetched".

One caveat for your n2vc_dma.c, not a blocker: n2vc_dma_guest_map(h, m, handle, bytes, &guest) carries no `pa`, so your registry cannot answer n2vc_dma_lookup_pa(pa) for buffers that *n2vcu* allocated through hw->dma_alloc (VCU_PA_ALLOCATION/MVA_ALLOCATION). n2vcu keeps its own table with (pa, guest, bytes) and resolves those internally, so nothing breaks. Your registry only has to answer lookup_pa for buffers *you* allocate, and guest_map/unmap/sync for every handle n2vcu passes you. The contract n2vcu relies on:
  - guest_map(handle, bytes) -> a guest address, and the SAME address if called again for the same handle;
  - guest_unmap(guest_addr) on free/close;
  - sync(guest_addr, off, len, to_device) copies through hw->dma_rw(handle, off, guest_ptr, len, to_device);
  - lookup_pa(pa) -> (handle, guest, bytes) for the pa-keyed buffers you allocate.
If you would rather have n2vcu pass the pa into a registration call, that is an include/n2vc_hw.h change and goes to hw-lead; my table already covers the model side, so I am not asking for it.
My test_v40 implements those four functions as a stand-in (in test_v40/t_v40.c) and will link your real n2vc_dma.c instead as soon as it is in the tree; ping me if the signatures end up different.
